# .NET Standards (C# 13, .NET 9)

## Naming Conventions

- **C# Code**: `PascalCase` for types, public members, methods, constants. `camelCase` for local variables, parameters, private fields (`_camelCase` for backing fields is optional but acceptable).
- **Tests (xUnit)**: Method name format must be `Should_<Outcome>_When_<Condition>` (PascalCase with underscores separating parts). Example: `Should_ReturnNotFound_When_ContactDoesNotExist`. If an SR-ID exists, use it as a prefix: `SR_CORE_API_01_Should_...` or set it in the `DisplayName`.
- **Database (Tables, Columns)**: **`snake_case`** is mandatory (Npgsql standard). Example: `user_contacts`, `created_at`.
- **Files and Folders**: `PascalCase` for C# files and project folders (matching namespaces). `kebab-case` for rules and configs (e.g., `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`).
- **Commits**: `feat: implement SR-ID` or `fix: description of the bug scenario`; keep the language consistent within the repository.

## Stack and Versions

- C# 13, .NET 9, ASP.NET Core, EF Core, Npgsql (PostgreSQL).
- EF Core Mapping: Strictly via **Fluent API** (`IEntityTypeConfiguration`). Avoid data annotations (attributes) on entities whenever possible.

## Project Structure

- Domain Entities -> `Models/`
- DbContext, Unit of Work -> `Data/`
- Repositories -> `Repositories/`
- Business Logic (Use Cases) -> `Services/`
- Validation -> FluentValidation in `Validation/`
- DTO Mapping -> `AutoMapProfiles/`
- gRPC -> `.proto` files in `Protos/`, interceptors in `Interceptors/`

## Architecture and Microservice Rules

- **NO REST CONTROLLERS**: This project is a Microservice, NOT an API Gateway. **DO NOT** create `Controllers/` folders or `[ApiController]` classes. All REST/SignalR entry points belong to the Gateway.
- **Thin gRPC layer**: The only entry points for this microservice are gRPC Services (`Grpc/`). These services must be extremely thin: receive request -> map DTOs -> call Use Case in `Services/` -> map results. **NO BUSINESS LOGIC IN GRPC SERVICES**.
- **Message Contracts**: RabbitMQ integration events and shared models MUST be placed in the `GrpcServiceName.Contracts` project so they can be distributed via NuGet.

## 🛑 STRICT PROHIBITIONS (NEVER DO THIS)

- **NEVER** use `DateTime.Now` or `DateTime.UtcNow`. ALWAYS use .NET 8/9 `TimeProvider` to allow for testing.
- **NEVER** throw `RpcException` inside `Services/` (business logic). Exceptions should only inherit from `DomainException` and must be converted to gRPC statuses in a global `Interceptor`.
- **NEVER** use `try-catch` inside Business Services for database failures or simple validations. Let exceptions bubble up.
- **NEVER** inject `IConfiguration` into Services. Always use `IOptions<T>` or `IOptionsSnapshot<T>`.
- **NEVER** inject `ServerCallContext` into `Services/`. The business layer knows nothing about gRPC.
- **NEVER** return `null` from Repositories. Either return a `Result<T>` pattern, use an `Optional`, or throw a domain exception (e.g. `NotFoundException`).

## The Golden Path (Use Case Pattern)

When implementing Business Logic inside the `Services/` folder, you **MUST** follow this style. 
Use `GrpcServiceTemplate/Services/ExampleService.cs` as your reference.

❌ **BAD** (Do NOT do this):
```csharp
public async Task<User> GetUser(int id, ServerCallContext ctx) {
    var user = _db.Users.Find(id); // Bad: DB context direct usage instead of repo pattern
    if(user == null) throw new RpcException(new Status(StatusCode.NotFound, "Not found")); // Bad: RpcException inside Service
    return user;
}
```

✅ **GOOD** (Golden Standard):
```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Получает сущность пользователя по его идентификатору.
/// </summary>
public async Task<User> GetUserAsync(int id, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default) 
{
    var user = await _repository.GetByIdAsync(id, cancellationToken);
    if (user is null) 
    {
        // Custom domain exception which will be intercepted globally 
        // and converted to StatusCode.NotFound
        throw new UserNotFoundException(id);
    }
    
    return user;
}
```

## Exception Handling and Boundaries

- Use Cases (`Services/`) must NOT depend on `Grpc.Core`, `ServerCallContext`, or throw `RpcException`.
- **Domain Exceptions**: The Business logic layer (`Services/`) MUST throw custom exceptions inheriting from `DomainException` (or use `Result<T>`) when business rules are violated (e.g., `ContactNotFoundException`, `ValidationException`).
- **Global Interceptors**: Mapping these domain errors to gRPC status codes MUST happen exclusively in the Transport layer via a global `Interceptor` (e.g., catching `DomainException` and converting it to `RpcException` with the correct `StatusCode`). Do NOT use `try-catch` blocks in gRPC service methods for this purpose.

## Style and Quality

- **Code Comments MUST be written in Russian.**
- Transactions: Explicitly manage transactions using `IDbContextTransaction` for complex operations.
- HttpClient: Always use `IHttpClientFactory`; configure resilience via `Polly`.
- Connections: Redis `ConnectionMultiplexer` MUST be a Singleton. RabbitMQ `IConnection` MUST be a Singleton; channels should be created correctly according to their lifecycle.
